more good news in the fight against  coronavirus this time from the uk  after 99 of volunteers given the oxford  astrozenica vaccine  developed antibodies to covert 19. these  are the results from the phase 2 trials  using 560 volunteers  results from the larger phase 3 trials  will be published in the next few weeks  but the powerful immune response it's  generating in adults over 60 is  particularly promising  as typically vaccines don't work as well  in older people  the editor of the scientific journal  that reviewed the data  says this vaccine is different to those  produced by moderna  and pfizer bioentech those vaccines are  based on  a technology called mrna  this vaccine is different because  the spike protein which is what provokes  the immune response  is bound to a recombinant adenoviral  vector it's a it's a virus which can  facilitate entry of the spike protein  into  the human cell the oxford vaccine had  been a front-runner in the global race  but was  overtaken by rivals moderna and pfizer  their phase 3 trials show more than 90  percent effectiveness  and they're now pressing ahead with  distribution plans  we have of course to wait uh to wait uh  um  for for authorization and approval yeah  if if everything goes well we could  start to  to to supply the vaccines to europe and  united states in the second half of  december  and and you can see that of course as a  christmas present  even once approved there will be huge  challenges in manufacturing and supply  chains  pfizer's vaccine needs to be stored at  -75 degrees celsius  which could hamper distribution  meanwhile countries are scrambling to  sign contracts  to secure doses for their population  china's synovac is already arriving in  brazil  the country hit hard by coronavirus is  importing some 46 million doses  to be used if it is approved charlie  andra aljazeera
 

